WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:06.960
Hello everyone.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.780
Welcome to the Langfocus channel,

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.260
and my name is Paul.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.120
Today's topic is, The Japanese language.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.240
Or ❝Nihongo (日本語)❞
as it's called in Japanese.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.360
Japanese has around 126 million
native speakers.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.440
Making it the 9th
most spoken language in the world,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.560
if we focus on native speakers alone.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.880
It's spoken mainly in the North-East
Asian nation of Japan.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.380
And also to a limited extent
in some emigrant communities

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.860
and among some elderly people
living in Japan's

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.700
former colonies like Korea and Taiwan.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.800
Japanese belongs to
the "Japonic" language family

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.840
which also includes the endangered
"Ryukyuan" languages

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.060
of Okinawa and the Amami Islands.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.100
But the early history of Japanese and

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.560
its relationship to other
languages is largely unknown.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.500
There are theories that
the Japanese and Korean languages

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.040
share a common ancestor.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.180
And there are theories
that Japanese and Korean

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.100
are part of a wider language family

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.840
called the Altaic language family.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.980
Which also includes the Turkic languages,

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.700
Mongolian, and the Tungusic languages.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.920
But, the Altaic language family,
and the connection between

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.040
Japanese and Korean are theoretic.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.980
They're hotly debated
and are not generally accepted.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.620
There are also theories,
that Japanese arose from

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.560
contact between the language
of the Yayoi people,

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.740
who migrated from
the North-East Asian mainland

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.020
Into Japan 2- 3000 years go.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.380
And the language or languages
of the Jomon people

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.160
who were already living there?

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.140
But, the nature of the language is
that these 2 groups of people

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.960
spoke is not clearly known?

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.020
Japanese was not a written language in

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.360
its early history, during the Yayoi period.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.540
Which leaves us very little
evidence of what it was

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.200
actually like during that time.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.540
In the 3rd century CE,
the Yamato state in Japan

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.180
established relations with China.
And the next 600 years

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.580
would be a period of
heavy Chinese influence.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.820
During which the rulers
and the elite thought that

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.640
emulates many aspects of
Chinese culture.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.480
Written Chinese was probably
introduced to Japan

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.660
in the 4th Century.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.020
And since Japanese had no written form,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.320
"Classical Chinese became
the first literary language" used by the elite.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.100
Later, "Chinese characters"
began being adapted to "write Japanese."

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.220
The earliest examples
we have of Japanese

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.140
writing are from the 8th century

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.520
and there are 2 forms.
Kanbun (漢文)

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.460
Kanbun (漢文)

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.040
And, Man'yōgana (万葉仮名)

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.020
Man'yōgana (万葉仮名)

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.960
kanbun was the writing of
Japanese in "Classical Chinese style,"

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.860
using Chinese characters to represent

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.600
the meaning of Japanese words.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.800
these texts were essentially Chinese,

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.460
but were "intended to be
readable in Japanese."

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.540
Man'yōgana was a way of
writing Japanese

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.820
entirely in Chinese characters, with

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.700
most of the "characters representing
the phonetic sound" of

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.960
the underlying Japanese syllables.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.400
For example,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.000
The Japanese word for
"mountain" - yama -

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.160
was written using
these 2 Chinese characters:

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.820
for their phonetic value,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.680
rather than using the Chinese character.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.960
Which means mountain,
which is the character used today?

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.300
And not only one Chinese character
was used for each sound.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.980
Numerous different characters could be

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.040
used to represent the same sound.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:05.740
For example,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.220
the syllables "ka" could be
represented by

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.200
any of the following characters:

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:14.100
Writing Japanese characters
phonetically using Chinese characters

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.120
that had no connection to
the meaning,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.940
must have felt like a lot of
unnecessary hard work.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.040
So, 2 systems of simplified
phonetic characters

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.240
developed from the
Man'yōgana system

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.000
in the 8th and 9th centuries

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.260
in order to simplify the writing process.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.220
These 2 systems were ❝Hiragana (ひらがな)❞
and ❝Katakana (カタカナ)❞

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.960
one of the main uses of these

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.880
new ❝Kana (仮名)❞ systems was to
annotate ❝Kanbun (漢文)❞ text.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.080
So, that Japanese speakers
could read these classical Chinese

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.120
or Chinese style texts
as though they were Japanese.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.360
Chinese characters represented
the meaning of the content words

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.620
while Kana provided the pronunciation,

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.080
as well as the grammatical
elements and inflections

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.100
that were not present in Chinese.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.840
Some diacritic marks also indicated how to 
change the word order

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.880
when reading the text in Japanese.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.020
This method of annotating
Chinese texts with "Kana"

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.460
was the precursor to
the Modern Japanese writing system.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.780
Through this process of
trying to make written Chinese language

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.740
fit with the spoken Japanese language,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.180
a huge amount of Chinese
vocabulary entered Japanese.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.880
Chinese characters were sometimes

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.260
annotated to be pronounced
with their Chinese pronunciation.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.040
Known as ❝ON-YOMI (音読み)❞ and
sometimes pronounced

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.720
as a native Japanese word
with a similar meaning.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.380
These pronunciations
are known as ❝KUN-YOMI (訓読み)❞

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.720
Such Chinese words have
become an integral part of Japanese,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.200
comprising 60% of the total
vocabulary (particularly in writing).

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.260
During the Late Middle Japanese period,

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.240
from the year 1185 to 1600.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.420
Japanese continued to
develop and moved

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.460
closer towards Modern Japanese

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.220
phonologically in particular.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:48.640
Towards the end of this time period,
In the year 1543

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.840
the first Europeans visited Japan.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.700
And some European vocabulary,
entered Japanese

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.560
through contact with traders and missionaries.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.800
This contact mostly came to a halt

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.400
during the ❝period of national isolation❞

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.000
from 1603-1853.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.540
During this time, contact with foreigners
was strictly limited to

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.000
a couple of specific places,
like the Dutch trading post

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.940
❝Dejima❞ in Nagasaki.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.080
A significant number of Dutch loanwords

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.240
did entered Japanese during this time period,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.500
some of them common everyday words.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.240
Like; "garasu (ガラス),"
from "glas."

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.120
"ranpu (ランプ),"
from "lamp."

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.140
"kōhī (コーヒー),"
from "koffie."

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.180
"koppu (コップ),"
from "kop" (cup).

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.740
These words are examples of
❝gairaigo (外来語)❞

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.520
loanwords aside from Chinese
borrowings and compounds

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.980
normally loanwords
from European languages.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.840
Which are normally written in Katakana.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.200
This period of isolation took place
during the Edo Period

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.240
during which the capital moved
from Kansai to Edo.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.480
Present-day Tokyo.
And the Edo dialect

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.060
became the standard variety of Japanese.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:52.680
After this period of isolation ended in 1853,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.720
interaction with the outside
world greatly increased.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.520
And this affected
the Japanese language

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.460
as it entered the Meiji Period.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.980
The number of gairaigo began to Increase.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.140
But even more than that

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:06.620
many new compound words
were created from Chinese characters,

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:09.120
and pronounced using
their Chinese pronunciation,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.800
the "ON-yomi" pronunciation.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.780
These new compounds words,
were created to

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.720
represent new Western concepts,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.120
as well as advanced
academic vocabulary in the arts,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.200
sciences, math, and technology.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.100
These new words that
were coined using Chinese characters

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.260
are referred to as 
"wasei kango (和製漢語)"

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.560
Which means something like,
"Japan-made Chinese words."

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.340
Since the end of World WarⅡ,

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.920
Japanese has adopted
a large number of gairaigo,

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.860
in particular from English.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.120
Some of these represent
everyday modern concepts, like;

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.440
コンピューター (konpyūtā)
"computer."

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.880
And others represent
specialized vocabulary of

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.900
academia and technology.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.800
During the Meiji period,
such specialized vocabulary

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.940
would have probably been
created from Chinese compounds.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.400
But in recent decades,
English has been a much bigger

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.160
source of new vocabularies.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.000
Varieties of Japanese.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.140
Standard Japanese today is
based on the dialect of Tokyo.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.700
But there are also
numerous unique and colorful

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:03.840
regional and local dialects as well.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.580
There are the distinctive
dialects of the Kansai region,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.540
which other Japanese people often
associate with comedy.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.400
Then there are the dialects of Tohoku

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.460
(which a lot of people have
trouble understanding).

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.400
And there are the dialects of Okinawa

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.400
which retains some
"elements of" the area's

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.040
old "Ryukyuan" languages.
And many more.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.840
Almost everywhere you go in Japan

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.240
there is a distinctive local dialect.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.220
The dialects of today are much
closer to Standard Japanese

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.840
than they were in the past.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.140
There are still some people
who speak full-on traditional dialects:

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:34.820
in particular older people,
working class people,

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.540
and people in the countryside.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.420
Basically, if someone meets
at least two of those criteria

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:41.500
that person probably uses much more

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.940
dialectal speech than a lot of other people.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:45.860
The majority of people speak something

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.360
close to Standard Japanese.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.260
But, with some elements
from the traditional dialect.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:52.540
And when speaking politely to strangers or

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:56.500
informal situations people
tend to avoid dialectal speech.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.440
So, what is Japanese like?

00:07:58.440 --> 00:07:59.420
phonology.

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:02.240
The basics of Japanese
phonology are quite simple.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.600
There are only 5 vowels in Japanese.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:05.340
あ
a

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:06.140
い
i

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:06.780
う
u

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:07.480
え
e

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:08.240
お
o

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.940
There are also long versions of these vowels.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:11.820
あー
ā

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:12.840
いー
ī

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:13.680
うー
ū

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.540
えー
ē

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:15.380
おー
ō

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:18.040
The distinction between
short and long vowels is

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.940
important to the meaning of words.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:23.680
Japanese vowels are always
fully articulated, and are not reduced

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.520
like they sometimes are in English.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.000
The consonants of
Japanese are for the most part

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.440
similar to those of English,

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.560
and cause few problems for learners.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.660
Japanese syllables basically all end in a vowel.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.680
This makes pronunciation fairly simple.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.960
Because there are a few consonant clusters.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:41.560
You normally don't have to pronounce

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.720
multiple consonants side by side.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.760
There are a couple of exceptions to this,

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:47.520
one is doubled consonants.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.020
For example the word,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.260
学校 "school"
gakkou

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.940
if we pronounce these
characters separately they are

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.480
ga"k"u + "k"ou

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.920
But because the consonants
in these 2 syllables, is the same,

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.860
they merge together, as
1 lengthened consonant.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.640
Written with a small letter, 2 between them,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.580
to indicate that you hold the consonant

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.040
for twice as long.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.240
you don't say "gaku-kou"

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.500
But, ga"kk"ou
が "っ" こう

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.900
another exception is
having a nasal sound

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:13.640
followed by another consonant.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.360
The nasal sound ん

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.940
Functions as its own syllable without a vowel.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:19.420
So, we have words like,

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:20.340
かんぱい
乾杯 (kanpai)

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:21.760
Which means, cheers.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:25.820
To be completely precise, each
Japanese Kana character,

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:29.680
represents a rhythmic unit
called a "mora", rather than a syllable.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:30.720
So, in the word;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.620
Ka-n-pa-i
かんぱい

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.700
There are 4 distinct, mora.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:35.900
But if we think of it in terms of syllables,

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:37.840
It has just 2 syllables.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:38.840
And in this word,

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:39.840
ga-k-ko-u
がっこう

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:41.400
There are 4 moras.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.580
But, in terms of syllables, it just has 2.

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:46.800
So, there are a couple of situations,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.120
when a syllable doesn't end in a vowel.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.920
But, for the most part,
syllables end in vowels.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.240
This becomes very clear,
when you look at some of the gairaigo

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.260
foreign loanwords in Japanese.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:00.000
in English there's "cake,"
but in Japanese it's―

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.880
ケーキ  (kēki)
With the vowel, "e" at the end.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.280
And notice the long vowel is
much more distinct in Japanese.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:10.280
Another example, in English
”hard disk" in Japanese―

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:11.780
ハードディスク  (hādodisuku)

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:13.840
And here's a bit of a wild example,

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.040
how do you say "McDonald's" in Japanese?

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:17.980
マクドナルド  (makudonarudo)

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.840
This aspect of Japanese phonology,
may make it a little bewildered to

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.320
use loanwords from English,
or other languages.

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.420
But it makes it easier to pronounce
native Japanese words.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:29.620
One aspect of Japanese phonology

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.560
that can be challenging for
learners is pitch accent.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.600
This is something that
a lot of learning materials

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.300
don't even mention at all.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.520
Japanese moras have either
a high pitch or a low pitch.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.880
With the high pitch representing
the accent of a word.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.360
There are cases when pitch accent indicates

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.100
a distinction and meaning.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:48.160
For example;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:49.080
はし
端

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:50.000
Meaning, "end."

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.440
This word has an accent, on the second mora.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:53.440
はし
橋 "bridge."

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.680
This word has an accent, on the first mora.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.560
And―

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:57.460
はし
箸

00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:58.780
Meaning, "chop-sticks."

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:01.600
And this word has an accent, on the second mora.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:05.940
Pitch accent also differs depending on
the dialect of the speaker.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.380
For learners, incorrect pitch accent rarely creates

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:10.800
problems for communication,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.600
but it does give you a distinct foreign accent.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:15.220
Word Order.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.200
The basic word order of Japanese is SOV.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.900
Subject - Object - Verb.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.760
With the verb coming at the end.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.460
In English we have,
Paul ate takoyaki.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.260
Or Paul ate (some) takoyaki.

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:28.680
But let's keep it simple for now,

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:30.400
in Japanese this sentence is―

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.240
ポールがたこ焼きを食べた
Pōru ga takoyaki o tabeta

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.460
Takoyaki by the way, 
are fried octopus dumplings.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:39.980
Here's the subject, followed by
the ❝subject marker=が(ga)❞

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:43.460
then the object, followed by
the ❝object marker=を(o)❞

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:44.820
and here's the verb.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.180
but in Japanese, various elements of
the sentence can be dropped

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:50.300
if they are obvious from the context.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:52.880
If you're speaking about yourself,
and you say

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:54.280
たこ焼きを食べた
takoyaki o tabeta

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.800
With no subjects,
this is understood as meaning,

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.300
"I ate takoyaki."

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.020
But if you're speaking
about your brother,

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:02.840
then it would be understood
as, "He ate Takoyaki."

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:05.460
Or if somebody asks, who ate the Takoyaki?

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:06.780
the answer might be―

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:07.960
私が食べた
watashi ga tabeta

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:09.280
Meaning, "I ate (it)."

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.420
Even though the object is not mentioned.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.960
Again, the object is obvious from the context.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.840
Or let's say you ask Micky.
Where did the Takoyaki go??

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.600
たこ焼きはどこに行ったの？
takoyaki wa doko ni itta no?

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:21.100
You'll probably say

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:21.900
食べた
tabeta

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.120
Meaning, "(I) ate (it)."

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.160
Even though there's no subject or object

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.260
they're obvious from the context.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:30.800
There are also equation all
sentences, that contain no verb,

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.720
but they end in a "verbal form."

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:35.140
A verbal form, can be a Noun + a copula

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.460
or an Adjective + a copula.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.660
イチローさんは、野球選手だ
Ichiro-san wa yakyū senshu da

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.440
This means, "Ichiro is a baseball player."

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.840
Here there's no verb,
just the Subject + Topic marker.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.480
Which will come back to you later,

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.040
than a noun, plus the copula "だ (da)."

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.820
この寿司屋さんは高いです
kono sushi ya-san wa takai desu

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:56.360
This means,
"This sushi shop is expensive."

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.660
here we have the Subject, Topic marker

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:01.420
then an adjective,
plus a copula "です (desu)."

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.300
notice that the copulas are different,

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:04.940
"だ (da)" is the "casual form,"

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:07.080
while "です (desu)" is
the "polite form."

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:09.380
if we want to say the second sentence casually,

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:10.960
we take away the copula.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:14.320
Since だ is not used after
adjectives, only nouns.

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:16.420
Topic marker and subject marker.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:19.720
Just now, we saw 2 special little words;

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.140
"は (wa)" and, "が (ga)"

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:24.840
"は (wa)" is the "topic marker," and
"が (ga)" is the "subject marker."

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:25.860
What's the difference?

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:29.300
They both seem to be placed after
the subject of the sentence, right?

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:32.540
Well, the topic marker は is used

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:34.080
when you want to
talk about something

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.060
that is already part of the conversation,

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:37.440
and make it the topic.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:40.120
So that everything else
in the sentence relates to it.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:41.360
Take this example.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.240
田中さんは優しい人です
Tanaka-san wa yasashī hito desu

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:45.720
This means,
❝Mr. Tanaka is a nice person.❞

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.480
The use of は in the sentence.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:50.920
Means that, Mr.Tanaka has already been
mentioned in the conversation.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:52.580
with something like the nuance of...

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:55.860
❝Speaking of Mr. Tanaka, he is a nice person.❞

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:58.440
The subject marker が, on the other hand,

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:01.560
is used when you introduce
something new to the conversation.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.360
今日先生が怒った
kyou sensei ga okotta

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.520
This means,
"The teacher got angry today."

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.680
Here, 先生 (sensei) the teacher
is new information,

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:10.200
this is the first time the teacher

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.020
has been mentioned in the conversation.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.360
By the way, in the sentence you
could add は (wa), after 今日 (kyou)

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.740
to make 今日 the topic of the sentence.

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:21.220
が also has a second important use,

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.000
which is to "focus" on something.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.500
For example, if you say

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.240
私がご飯を作る
watashi ga gohan o tsukuru

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:28.000
That means,
"I'll cook a meal."

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.620
With emphasis on "I".

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:33.120
In English, we do this by stressing
and increasing the volume of a word.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.200
Like, I'll cook a meal.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:38.880
To be honest, I prefer to think of
が as a focus marker

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:40.680
because of the use I just mentioned

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.560
and also because,
it can be used to mark the "object"

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:45.380
when the object is stressed.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:46.800
ドーナツが食べたい
dōnatsu ga tabetai

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:49.260
Which means.
❝I want to eat donuts.❞

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.860
With the emphasis on "Donuts."

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:54.200
Or you could think of it like
❝Donuts are what I want to eat.❞

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.700
が is also used to mark
the object of certain stative verbs.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:14:59.740
For example,

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:01.500
彼は英語が出来る
kare wa eigo ga dekiru

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:05.380
in this sentence 英語 (eigo) is the object,
and the subject is 彼 (kare)

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:07.100
And it's also the topic.

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:09.860
In which case, は is used instead of が

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.800
There are also some other sentences
in which what seems to be

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.280
the object is actually the subject
and is marked by が

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.260
For example,

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:19.660
りんごが欲しい
ringo ga hoshī

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:21.260
which means,
❝I want an apple.❞

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:23.840
Word-for-word It's apple-subject marker-

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:25.680
or focus marker want.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:29.700
in the sentence, " Hoshī " is actually
an adjective meaning "desirable,"

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.160
so the subject is "ringo."

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:34.420
But learners would often mistake
ringo for the object.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.980
And try to use the object marker を (o)

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:41.040
Japanese has numerous other
particles aside from は and が

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:43.640
Particles are words that
indicate the relationship of

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:45.540
a word, or a phrase or a clause,

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:46.880
to the rest of the sentence.

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:49.260
They always come after the word
they're connected to.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:52.620
Some show grammatical function of
the word like は (wa)  が (ga)

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.200
and the object marker を (o),

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.220
while others function like prepositions.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:56.920
For example,

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.740
から (kara) meaning "from"
に (ni) meaning "to"

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:03.260
and, の (no) meaning "of"
or showing possession.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.540
Agglutination.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:09.860
Japanese is to a large extent
and "agglutinative" language

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.760
(particularly in its verbs)

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.460
In agglutinative languages,
words have a relatively

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:16.340
high number of morphemes.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:20.200
Rather than expressing an additional idea
by adding an extra word,

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.380
you can add an additional affix
to an existing word.

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:24.960
Let's say, this simple verb.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:25.780
怒る
okoru

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.220
which means, "to get angry."

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:31.360
"okoru" is the "stem,"
and "u" is the "dictionary form ending."

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:35.160
Which we can take away,
we can add various suffixes to the stem

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:36.740
to add to its core meaning.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:37.720
怒られる
okorareru

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:40.120
this is the passive form of ❝get angry.❞

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:42.660
And it can be translated as "to be scolded."

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.360
Let's get rid of the る (ru)
and add た (ta)

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:46.520
怒られた
okorareta

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:48.720
This means, "I got scolded" or,

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.260
"(Someone) got angry at me."

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:52.880
And note that this is just
a verb, with no pronouns.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:55.820
But who you're talking about
would be clear from the context.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:58.320
た (ta) indicates the past tense.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:01.680
Now, let's go back to the stem,
and add a different suffix

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.060
"aseru" which creates the causative form.

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:05.120
怒らせる
okoraseru

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:07.100
meaning, ❝to make (someone) angry.❞

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:10.020
Now, let's take this る (ru)
away and add this;

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.280
怒らせたい
okorase"tai" → "want to."

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:13.980
This means,
❝(I) want to make (someone) angry.❞

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:16.520
Just from this word,
we don't know who someone is,

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.960
but the object would be
clear from the context.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:20.460
Even though the meaning is weird

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.180
let's say this means,
❝I want to make you angry.❞

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:25.620
Now, let's make this a negative, to do that

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:28.280
we take off い (i), and
replace it with く (ku),

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:31.140
to make a connecting form
then we add ない (nai)

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:32.540
怒らせたく "ない"
okorasetaku"nai"

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:34.780
This means,
❝I "don't" want to make you angry.❞

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.900
And again, the you part depends on the context.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:39.620
Now let's make it past tense,

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:41.080
怒らせたく "なかった"
okorasetaku"nakatta"

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:43.660
This means,
❝I "didn't" want to make you angry.❞

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.340
なかった (nakatta) is the past tense
form of ない (nai)

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.740
with た (ta) indicating the past tense.

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:52.220
So, you can see that Japanese verbs
are highly agglutinative.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:54.760
Vocabulary and
the writing system.

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:58.220
As I mentioned before when
talking about the history of Japanese.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.740
Japanese vocabulary is a combination of:

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:03.160
Native Japanese words for 和語 (wago)

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:05.420
Chinese loanwords or words created from

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:07.580
Chinese characters 漢語 (kango)

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:11.160
and loanwords from
other languages or 外来語 (gairaigo)

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:13.880
A typical Japanese sentence includes

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:16.920
Kanji or Chinese characters,
as well as Hiragana.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.320
One of the 2 Kana syllabaries
that I mentioned earlier.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.620
The Chinese characters
sometimes represent kango.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:26.440
So, the Kanji are pronounced
with their ON-yomi.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:29.040
But they sometimes represent
native Japanese words

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.040
pronounced with their KUN-yomi.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:34.900
Some native Japanese words
are also written in Hiragana.

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:36.720
But Hiragana is mainly used for

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:38.940
"writing the grammatical
elements of the sentence,"

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.300
such as particles, and inflections.

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:43.760
私は今日学校に行きたくない。
watashi wa kyou gakkou ni ikitakunai

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:46.400
This means,
❝I don't want to go to school today.❞

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:47.420
Word for word is,

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:48.840
I-topic marker-

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.160
today-school-

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:52.240
to-don't want to go.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.280
Here let's start with the verb,

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:56.280
its dictionary form is iku (行く)

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:59.300
ikitakunai (行きたくない)
means, don't want to go.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:02.340
The Chinese character here
represents the meaning "go."

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:06.740
But phonetically it only represents
the first syllable i (い) and Ki (き)

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.100
comes after the kanji.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:11.840
That's because this sound changes
depending on the form of the verb.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:13.900
The way that suffixes are added to

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:16.280
the stem, is meant to be read in Hiragana.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:18.660
This use of Hiragana to show the inflections

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:22.020
and part of a kanji's pronunciation is
called  "okurigana."

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:24.440
Moving back a little we have 学校 (gakkou)

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:27.260
this is a kango a Chinese compound.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:30.220
It's followed by the particle
に (ni) meaning, "to."

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:33.100
Then here we have 今日 (kyou)
another kango,

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:34.720
then we have 私 (watashi)

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:38.220
Which is a native Japanese word,
represented by a Chinese character

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.820
using it kun-yomi pronunciation.

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:43.200
Let me pause for a little sidenote here.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:45.740
I've been saying ❝on-yomi❞ and
❝kun-yomi❞ as though

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:48.040
there's just one a reach for every kanji,

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:49.500
but it's not really that simple.

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:52.700
There are often multiple ON-yomi
readings and multiple kun-yomi

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.400
readings for a single character.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:55.700
And some of them have additional

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:58.380
irregular pronunciations
when used in names.

00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:01.360
Which pronunciation you use
depends on the context.

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:02.040
For example;

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:03.920
when you see okurigana
you know that

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:06.820
you're using a kun-yomi,
and not an ON-yomi.

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:08.820
And a quick sidenote about kango.

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:11.020
Many kango can be turned into verbs

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:13.960
by adding the word, "suru" meaning, "to do."

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:14.740
For example,

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:15.800
料理
ryouri

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.040
Which means, "cuisine."

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:17.760
and

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:18.720
料理 "する"
ryouri "suru"

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:21.540
Which means, "to make food" / "to cook."

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:23.040
協力
kyouryoku

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.240
Means, cooperation.

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:24.800
And―

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:25.740
協力 "する"
kyouryoku "suru"

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.120
Means, "to cooperate."

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.120
Some Japanese sentences
also contain "katakana,"

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:33.320
usually to represent "外来語" (gairaigo)

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:35.320
foreign loanwords that aren't kango.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:37.840
"カメラ"の使い方が分からない
"kamera" no tsukaikata ga wakaranai

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.920
This means,
❝ I don't know how to use the "camera" ❞

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:43.560
word-for-word its
camera-possessive particle-

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:47.360
use-way-subject marker-don't know.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:50.280
Here we see the word
カメラ written in katakana.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.200
Then we see the kanji
meaning "use"

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.440
followed by okurigana い (i)

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:56.820
So we know it's a kun-yomi pronunciation.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:59.020
Then there's "方 (kata)"
which means, "way."

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:01.940
So, tsukaikata means, "way of using."

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:04.820
Then we see the subject marker or focus marker

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:08.900
in this case, marking the object
then we see "wakaranai"

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.700
Which means, "I don't know."

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:13.300
Again, this is a chinese character
with okurigana.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:15.560
There are no kango in this sentence.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:18.860
The 2 syllabaries, hiragana and katakana,

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.520
are more or less two versions
of the same script.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:24.960
Each letter in either alphabet has
an equivalent In the other.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.060
And they often look fairly similar,

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:29.660
except that katakana
tends to be more angular,

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:31.920
while Hiragana is rounder.

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:35.100
"Why do some Hiragana and Katakana pairs

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:37.580
look so similar while others don't?"

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:39.580
Well, if you remember before,

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:42.220
the kana syllabaries, developed from Kanji.

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.900
In some cases, Hiragana and
Katakana characters

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:47.900
developed from the same kanji.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:51.780
But In other cases they
developed from different kanji.

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:56.420
The kana syllabaries are quite easy to learn.

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:59.560
But as learners of Japanese
inevitably discovered

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:01.820
reading a sentence written entirely in kana

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:05.160
is actually harder than reading
a sentence that contains kanji

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:06.700
provided that you know the kanji.

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:09.980
That's because kanji give you
an immediate visual cue of a word.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:12.200
So you understand the meaning
more quickly than

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:15.180
if you have to phonetically
pronounce all the kana.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:19.900
As you can see Japanese is
a fascinating multifaceted language.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:23.060
In fact, there are so many different
interesting aspects to the language

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:24.680
that people feel intimidated,

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:26.920
because they think that
they can't learn everything.

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.220
Well, who cares if you learn everything?

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.520
You don't need to know
2,000 kanji characters

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.240
or be really good with
honorifics in order to,

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:36.360
talk and make friends with
Japanese speakers.

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:39.740
Or to enjoy Japanese Manga,
animation, and music.

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:42.180
Japanese is a very rewarding language to learn

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:44.460
even at a basic or intermediate level.

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:46.720
And the richness of the language
will provide you

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.560
with countless treasures to discover

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:51.040
no matter how far you go with the language.

